From: SamuriHL <samurihl@msn.com>
Subject: bdj: return from selectLanguage() once the stream is selected

StreamControl.selectLanguage() finds the stream whose language matches, selects
it, and then keeps iterating - and falls out of the loop into the unconditional
`throw new LanguageNotAvailableException()` at the end. So a call that SUCCEEDS
still throws, and any Xlet selecting audio or subtitles by language name sees an
exception it has no reason to expect.

Two consequences, both real:

  - selectLanguage() always throws, success or not. An Xlet that treats the
    exception as fatal abandons whatever it was doing, and BD-J titles do
    exactly that inside stream-setup handlers.

  - selectDefaultLanguage() calls selectLanguage() and converts
    LanguageNotAvailableException into NotAuthorizedException, so it always
    throws too - including on the ordinary path where the disc's default
    language was found and selected.

There is a second, quieter bug in the same loop: without the return, a later
stream carrying the same language code overwrites the selection, so the LAST
match wins where the API contract implies the first.

Add the return. Nothing else changes: a genuine no-match still reaches the
LanguageNotAvailableException, and a match whose stream number is out of range
still converts to NotAuthorizedException.
--- a/src/libbluray/bdj/java/org/videolan/media/content/playlist/StreamControl.java
+++ b/src/libbluray/bdj/java/org/videolan/media/content/playlist/StreamControl.java
@@ -117,6 +117,7 @@
             if (streams[i].getLang().equals(language)) {
                 try {
                     selectStreamNumber(i + 1);
+                    return;
                 } catch (StreamNotAvailableException e) {
                     throw new NotAuthorizedException();
                 }
